قَوْلِهِ تَعالى: ﴿وَكَمْ أهْلَكْنا مِنَ القُرُونِ مِن بَعْدِ نُوحٍ وكَفى بِرَبِّكَ بِذُنُوبِ عِبادِهِ خَبِيرًا بَصِيرًا﴾ .

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And how many generations have We destroyed after Noah? And sufficient is your Lord, as Knower and Seer of the sins of His servants.﴾ He, may He be exalted, mentions in this noble verse that He destroyed many generations after Noah. The word *kam* ("how many") in His saying ﴿And how many We have destroyed﴾ [al-Isra: 17] is *khabariyyah* (declarative), meaning that it conveys information about a large number. It also conveys that He, may He be exalted, is the All-Aware, the All-Seeing, of the sins of His servants. He confirmed this with His saying ﴿And sufficient is your Lord﴾ etc. [al-Isra: 17].

What this noble verse indicates is clarified by other verses in four respects:

The first: The verse contains a warning to the disbelievers of Makkah and a frightening of them lest there befall them what befell other nations that denied their messengers. That is, "We destroyed many generations after Noah because of their denial of the messengers, so do not deny Our Messenger, lest We do to you the like of what We did to them."

The verses that clarify this meaning are many. Among them:

• His saying concerning the people of Lot: ﴿And indeed, you pass by them in the morning, and at night. Will you not then reason?﴾ [al-Saffat: 137-138];

• His saying concerning them also: ﴿Indeed, in that are signs for those who discern. And indeed, it is on a road that remains.﴾ [al-Hijr: 75-76];

• His saying concerning them also: ﴿And We have left of it a clear sign for a people who reason.﴾ [al-Ankabut: 35];

• His saying: ﴿Have they not traveled through the earth and observed how was the end of those before them? Allah destroyed them, and for the disbelievers is the like thereof.﴾ [Muhammad: 10];

• His saying, after His mention, may He be exalted, of His destruction of the people of Noah, the people of Hud, the people of (p-٨٠) Salih, the people of Lot, and the people of Shu'ayb, in Surat ash-Shu'ara': Indeed, ﴿in that is a sign, but most of them were not believers﴾ [al-Shu'ara: 8];

• His saying concerning the people of Musa: ﴿Indeed, in that is a lesson for those who fear﴾ [al-Nazi'at: 26];

• His saying: ﴿Indeed, in that is a sign for whoever fears the punishment of the Hereafter﴾;

• His saying: ﴿Are they better than the people of Tubba' and those before them? We destroyed them﴾ etc. [al-Dukhan: 37];

in addition to many other verses that indicate their being frightened by what befell those before them.

The second respect: These generations were the subject of explanation in other verses. They clarified how the people of Noah, the people of Hud, the people of Salih, the people of Lot, the people of Shu'ayb, and Pharaoh and his people from the people of Musa were destroyed. This is mentioned in multiple well-known places in the Book of Allah, the Exalted. And He made clear that those generations were numerous in His saying: ﴿and 'Ad and Thamud and the companions of ar-Rass and generations between them, many﴾ [al-Furqan: 38]. And He explained in another place that among them are those whom none knows except Allah, the Exalted. That is in His saying in Surat Ibrahim: ﴿Has there not come to you the news of those before you: the people of Noah, 'Ad, and Thamud, and those after them, whom none knows except Allah?﴾ etc. [Ibrahim: 9]. And He explained in two other places that among their messengers were some whose story He related to our Prophet (ﷺ) and some whose story He did not relate to him. These are His saying in Surat an-Nisa': ﴿And messengers We have related to you before, and messengers We have not related to you; and Allah spoke to Musa directly﴾ [al-Nisa: 164], and His saying in Surat al-Mu'min: ﴿And We had already sent messengers before you; among them are those We have related to you, and among them are those We have not related to you. And it was not for any messenger to bring a sign except by permission of Allah﴾ etc. [Ghafir: 78].

The third respect: His saying ﴿after Noah﴾ [al-Isra: 17] indicates that the generations that existed between Adam and Noah were upon Islam, as Ibn 'Abbas said: "Between Adam and Noah there were ten generations, all of them upon Islam." Ibn Kathir transmitted this from him in his tafsir of this verse.

This meaning is indicated by other verses, such as His saying: ﴿Mankind was one community, then Allah sent the prophets as bearers of good tidings and warners﴾ etc. [al-Baqarah: 213], and His saying: ﴿And mankind was not but one community, and they differed﴾ etc. [Yunus: 19]. This is because the meaning of these, according to the most correct opinions, is that they were upon the way of Islam, until there occurred what occurred from the people of Noah of disbelief. So Allah sent the prophets forbidding that disbelief, giving good tidings to whoever obeyed them of Paradise, and warning whoever disobeyed them of the Fire. The first of them in that was Noah, upon him and upon our Prophet be prayers and peace.

(p-٨١) This is indicated by His saying: ﴿Indeed, We have revealed to you as We revealed to Noah and the prophets after him﴾ etc. [al-Nisa: 163]. And in the hadiths of intercession, which are established in the Sahih collections and others, they say to Noah: "Indeed, you are the first messenger Allah sent to the people of the earth," as we mentioned in Surat al-Baqarah.

The fourth respect: His saying ﴿And sufficient is your Lord, as Knower and Seer of the sins of His servants﴾ [al-Isra: 17] contains the greatest deterrent from committing what does not please Allah, the Exalted.

The verses that clarify this are very many:

• His saying: ﴿And We have created man and We know what his soul whispers to him, and We are closer to him than his jugular vein﴾ [Qaf: 16];

• His saying: ﴿Unquestionably, they fold their chests to hide from Him. Unquestionably, when they cover themselves with their garments, He knows what they conceal and what they declare. Indeed, He is Knowing of that within the breasts﴾ [Hud: 5];

• His saying: ﴿And know that Allah knows what is in your souls, so beware of Him﴾ [al-Baqarah: 235];

in addition to other verses.

We have already presented this topic in an explanation at the beginning of Surat Hud. The word *kam* ("how many") in this noble verse is in the position of the object of *ahlakna* ("We destroyed"). And *min* in His saying ﴿mina al-qurun﴾ ("from the generations") is an explanation of *kam* and a specification of it, just as a number is specified by its kind. As for the word *min* in His saying ﴿min ba'di Nuh﴾ ("after Noah"), the apparent view is that it denotes the starting point of the limit (*ibtida' al-ghayah*), and this is what Abu Hayyan chose in *al-Bahr*. Al-Hawfi claimed that the second *min* is a substitute (*badal*) for the first, and Abu Hayyan refuted him on this. And knowledge is with Allah, the Exalted.

The Words of the Most High: ﴿And We gave Thamud the she-camel as a clear sign, but they wronged themselves through it﴾ – the verse.

In this noble verse, the Majestic and Exalted explains that He gave Thamud the she-camel as a sign that gives sight (mubsirah), that is, a clear proof that makes them see the truth plainly, with no confusion in it. "But they wronged themselves through it." He does not explain here the nature of their wrongdoing, but He clarifies it in other places, such as:

• His saying: ﴿So they hamstrung the she-camel and defied the command of their Lord﴾ – the verse [al-A'raf: 77];

• His saying: ﴿But they denied him and hamstrung her﴾ – the verse [ash-Shams: 14];

• His saying: ﴿Then they called their companion, and he took (a sword) and hamstrung her﴾ [al-Qamar: 29];

and other verses besides these.

He said, may He be exalted: ﴿They say, "We will not believe you until you cause a spring to gush forth for us from the earth,"﴾ ﴿"or you have a garden of palm trees and grapevines and cause rivers to gush forth within it abundantly,"﴾ ﴿"or you cause the sky to fall upon us in pieces, as you claimed, or you bring God and the angels before us as a guarantee,"﴾ ﴿"or you have a house of gold, or you ascend into the sky. And we will not believe in your ascension until you send down to us a book that we can read." Say, "Glory be to my Lord! Am I anything but a human messenger?"﴾. In these noble verses God, exalted and high, made clear the extreme obstinacy and stubbornness of the disbelievers and the great number of their demands, which were made for the sake of stubbornness and not for the sake of seeking the truth. He mentioned that they said to him (ﷺ) that they would not believe in him, meaning they would not accept him as truthful, until he caused a spring to gush forth for them from the earth. Yanbu' (spring) is on the pattern yafu'ul, from nabaʿa (to well up), meaning abundant water. From the same root is God's saying: ﴿So He makes it flow as springs in the earth﴾ (p-184) [al-Zumar: 21]. ﴿Or you have a garden﴾ [al-Isra': 91], meaning an orchard of palm trees and grapevines, and you cause rivers of water to gush forth within it, meaning in its midst. Or you cause the sky to fall upon them in pieces, meaning fragments, as he claimed, that is, in God's saying: ﴿If We will, We shall cause the earth to swallow them, or cause pieces of the sky to fall upon them﴾ [Saba': 9], or he brings them God and the angels as a guarantee, meaning face to face (mu'ayanah, direct visual witnessing). This was said by Qatadah and Ibn Jurayj, like His saying: ﴿Why are angels not sent down to us, or do we not see our Lord?﴾ [al-Furqan: 21].

Some scholars said: qabilan means kafilan (a guarantor), from taqabbalahu bi-kadha, meaning when one stands surety for it. Qabil, kafil and za'im (guarantor) have the same meaning. Al-Zamakhshari said: qabilan means a witness to its truth. That qabil in this verse means kafil is related from Ibn ʿAbbas and al-Dahhak. Muqatil said: qabilan means witness. Mujahid said: it is the plural of qabilah (tribe), meaning that he brings the various kinds of angels, and on this view it is a circumstantial clause (hal) referring to the angels. ﴿Or you have a house of gold﴾, meaning of gold (dhahab). From the same usage is His saying in az-Zukhruf: ﴿And had it not been that people would become one community, We would have made for those who disbelieve in the Most Merciful silver roofs for their houses﴾ up to His saying ﴿and ornaments of gold﴾ [az-Zukhruf: 33–35], meaning gold. ﴿Or he ascends into the sky﴾, meaning he climbs up into it. They said they would not believe in his ascent, meaning on account of his going up, until he sends down to them a book they can read. This extreme stubbornness and great obstinacy that God, exalted and high, mentioned here about the disbelievers, He clarified in other places, and He made clear that even if God did what they proposed, they would not believe, because whoever is destined for wretchedness will not believe;

• like His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And even if We had sent down to you a book on paper and they had touched it with their hands, those who disbelieved would have said, "This is nothing but clear magic"﴾ [al-An'am: 7];

• and His saying: ﴿Even if We had sent down to them the angels and the dead had spoken to them and We had gathered everything before them face to face, they would not have believed unless God willed﴾ [al-An'am: 111];

• and His saying: ﴿And if We opened for them a gate from the sky and they continued to ascend through it, they would surely say, "Our eyes have only been dazzled; rather, we are a people bewitched"﴾ [al-Hijr: 14–15];

• and His saying: ﴿And what would make you realize that when it comes they will not believe?﴾ [al-An'am: 109];

• and His saying: ﴿Indeed, those upon whom the word of your Lord has come into effect will not believe, even if every sign should come to them, until they see the painful punishment﴾ [Yunus: 96–97].

Verses similar to these are many.

His saying in this verse: ﴿a book that we can read﴾, meaning a book from God to each man among us.

This is clarified by His saying in al-Muddaththir: ﴿Rather, every man among them wants to be given scrolls spread open﴾ (p-185) [al-Muddaththir: 52], as His saying indicates: ﴿And when a sign comes to them, they say, "We will not believe until we are given what was given to the messengers of God"﴾ [al-An'am: 124].

His saying in this noble verse: ﴿Say, "Glory be to my Lord! Am I anything but a human messenger?"﴾ [al-Isra': 93], meaning: exalting my Lord, exalted and high, above everything unfitting for Him. This includes exalting Him above incapacity to do what you have proposed; He is able over all things, and nothing can overpower Him. I am a human being who follows what my Lord reveals to me.

This meaning is explained in other places, such as His saying: ﴿Say, "I am only a human being like you, to whom it is revealed that your god is but one God. So whoever hopes for the meeting with his Lord, let him do righteous work and not associate anyone in the worship of his Lord"﴾ [al-Kahf: 110], and His saying: ﴿Say, "I am only a human being like you, to whom it is revealed that your god is but one God, so take the straight path to Him and seek His forgiveness"﴾ [Fussilat: 6]; and His saying about all the messengers: ﴿Their messengers said to them, "We are only human beings like you, but God bestows His favor upon whom He wills of His servants"﴾ [Ibrahim: 11], and other verses.

As for the readings: Ta-fjura in the first instance is read by ʿAsim, Hamzah and al-Kisa'i with a fatha on the ta and sukun on the fa and damma on the jim; the rest read with damma on the ta, fatha on the fa and shaddah on the jim with kasra. All agreed on the reading in the second instance. Nafi', Ibn ʿAmir and ʿAsim read kisafan with fatha on the sin, and the rest with sukun on it. Abu ʿAmr read tunzila with sukun on the nun and lightening of the zay, and the rest with fatha on the nun and shaddah on the zay.

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 17, verse 99:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿Do they not see that Allah, who created the heavens and the earth, is able to create the likes of them?﴾.

In this noble verse, exalted and most high is He, He makes clear that the One who created the heavens and the earth, despite their greatness, is undoubtedly able to resurrect humankind; for whoever created the greater and the greatest is undoubtedly able to create the lesser.

He clarified this meaning in other places:

• As in His saying: ﴿The creation of the heavens and the earth is greater than the creation of mankind﴾ [Ghafir: 57], meaning: whoever is able to create the greater is able to create the lesser;

• And His saying: ﴿Is not He who created the heavens and the earth able to create the likes of them? Yes indeed﴾ [Ya-Sin: 81];

• And His saying: ﴿Do they not see that Allah, who created the heavens and the earth and was not wearied by their creation, is able to give life to the dead? Yes indeed﴾ [al-Ahqaf: 33];

• And His saying: ﴿Are you more difficult to create, or is the heaven which He constructed? He raised its height and made it proportioned. And He darkened its night and brought out its forenoon. And the earth — after that He spread it out. He extracted from it its water and its pasture. And the mountains He set firmly, as enjoyment for you and your grazing livestock﴾ [al-Nazi'at: 27–33].

The Words of Allah, Exalted is He: ﴿And We certainly gave Moses nine clear signs﴾

...the verse. Some of the people of knowledge said: These nine signs are: the staff, the hand, the years of drought (al-sinin), the sea, the flood, the locusts, the lice (al-qummal), the frogs, and the blood, as distinct signs (ayat mufassalat).

Allah, Exalted and Most High, has explained these signs in other places, such as:

• His saying: ﴿So he cast his staff, and behold, it was a clearly visible serpent﴾ ﴿And he drew out his hand, and behold, it was white for the onlookers﴾ [al-A'raf: 107-108];

• His saying: ﴿And We certainly seized the people of Pharaoh with years of drought and a lack of fruits﴾ to the end of the verse [al-A'raf: 130];

• His saying: ﴿So We revealed to Moses: Strike the sea with your staff, and it parted, and each part was like a mighty mountain﴾ [al-Shu'ara': 63];

• His saying: ﴿So We sent upon them the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs, and the blood, as distinct signs﴾ [al-A'raf: 133], and other signs that clarify what we have mentioned. Some of them substituted the mountain for "the years" (al-sinin), and in that case the saying of Allah, the Exalted, ﴿And when We raised the mountain above them as though it were a canopy﴾ [al-A'raf: 171 / 117 in some numbering], explains that,

and similar verses.

Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 17, verse 111:

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And say, "All praise is due to Allah, who has not taken a son and has not had a partner in dominion, nor has He any ally out of weakness; so glorify Him with [complete] glorification."﴾

In this noble verse, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commands mankind, through the tongue of His Prophet ﷺ (because the command to the exemplar is a command to his followers, as we have already mentioned), to say: "Al-hamdu lillah" (All praise is due to Allah), meaning: every beautiful praise befitting His perfection and majesty belongs to Him, declaring that He is free of having children, partners, and needing to be strengthened by allies, exalted be He above all of that with a great exaltation.

He has made clear His transcendence above having a son or a consort in many places, such as:

• His saying: ﴿Say, "He is Allah, [who is] One"﴾ [al-Ikhlas: 1] to the end of the surah;

• His saying: ﴿And [it is] that the majesty of our Lord has not taken a companion or a son﴾ [al-Jinn: 3];

• His saying: ﴿Originator of the heavens and the earth. How could He have a son when He does not have a companion and He created all things, and He is, of all things, Knowing﴾ [al-An'am: 101];

• His saying: ﴿And they say, "The Most Merciful has taken a son."﴾ ﴿You have certainly produced [something] imposing.﴾ ﴿The heavens are about to burst from it, and the earth split open, and the mountains collapse in devastation,﴾ ﴿That they attribute to the Most Merciful a son.﴾ ﴿And it is not appropriate for the Most Merciful to take a son.﴾ [Maryam: 88–92], and the verses.

Verses of this kind are many.

And He has made clear in other places that He has no partner in His dominion, that is, nor in His worship;

• such as His saying: ﴿And for them therein is no partnership, and not for Him is there among them any supporter﴾ [Saba': 22];

• and His saying: ﴿"Whose is the dominion this Day?" "For Allah, the One, the Subduer."﴾ [Ghafir: 16];

• and His saying: ﴿Blessed is He in whose hand is dominion, and He is over all things competent﴾ [al-Mulk: 1];

• and His saying: ﴿Say, "O Allah, Owner of Sovereignty, You give sovereignty to whom You will and remove sovereignty from whom You will. You honor whom You will and You degrade whom You will"﴾ [Al 'Imran: 26], and the verses.

Verses of this kind are many.

As for His saying in this verse: ﴿nor has He any ally out of weakness﴾ [al-Isra': 111], it means that He is not humbled so as to need an ally by whom He would be strengthened; for He is the Mighty, the Subduer, to whose subjugation and power everything is subject, as He has made clear in many places,

• such as His saying: ﴿And Allah prevails over His affair﴾ [Yusuf: 21], and the verse;

• and His saying: ﴿Indeed, Allah is Mighty and Wise﴾ [al-Baqarah: 209], where "al-'Aziz" means the Dominant;

• and His saying: ﴿And He is the Subduer over His servants﴾ [al-An'am: 18];

and the verses of this kind are many.

As for His saying ﴿and glorify Him with [complete] glorification﴾ [al-Isra': 111], that is, magnify Him with intense magnification. The magnification of Allah becomes manifest in strictly guarding the carrying out of His command and avoiding His prohibition, and in hastening to everything that pleases Him, as in His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿And that you may glorify Allah for that to which He has guided you﴾ [al-Baqarah: 185], and similar verses. And knowledge is with Allah, may He be exalted.

Ibn Jarir narrated in his tafsir about this noble verse, from Qatadah, that he said: It was mentioned to us that "the Prophet ﷺ used to teach the young and the old among his family this verse: ﴿All praise is due to Allah, who has not taken a son﴾" to the end of the verse [al-Isra': 111]. Ibn Kathir said: I say: It has come in a hadith that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named this verse "the verse of might" (ayat al-'izz), and in some reports: it is not recited in a house on a night, and a theft or a calamity befalls it. And Allah knows best. Then he mentioned a hadith from Abu Ya'la, from the hadith of Abu Hurairah, whose implication is that reciting this verse removes illness and harm. Then he said: its chain is weak, and its text contains a strangeness (nakarah). And Allah, may He be exalted, knows best.

May Allah send blessings upon our Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.

This is the end of the third part of this blessed book; the fourth part follows it, Allah willing, and its beginning is Surah al-Kahf. And success is from Allah.
